## Bringing a design in

A design arrives as a PDF, a picture or a Figma link. `design import` turns each into reference images the agent is shown, saved with a record
(`references.json`: what each file is, where it came from, its size and checksum) that `design check` verifies, so a reference cannot change unnoticed.

```bash
./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc design import mockups.pdf --feature FEAT-001            # every page (up to 20) becomes a picture, plus the text in the PDF
./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc design import login.png signup.png --feature FEAT-001   # PNG, JPEG, WebP or GIF, up to 15 MB each
export FIGMA_TOKEN=...                                                     # a personal access token, scope file_content:read; PowerShell: $env:FIGMA_TOKEN = "..."
./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc design import "https://www.figma.com/design/<key>/<name>?node-id=1-2" --feature FEAT-001
./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc design import brand-guide.pdf --project                  # a style guide for the whole project: .ai/design/references/
```

- **Where**: `--feature FEAT-001` saves in that feature's `design/` folder (next to its PRD, read-only while an agent heals); `--project` saves in `.ai/design/references/`.
- **PDF**: pages are rendered with poppler's `pdftoppm` (macOS: `brew install poppler`; Ubuntu: `sudo apt install poppler-utils`; Windows: `scoop install poppler` or `choco install poppler`). Without it, macOS can render the first page only, at 72 dpi. `--pages 3-5` and `--dpi 200` choose pages and sharpness. The text found in the PDF is saved as a `.txt`.
- **Figma**: the link's frame (or the frames of a page or section) is exported as a PNG, with its text in reading order (`.txt`) and a census of the colours, type sizes, radii and spacing it uses, to help you write the tokens. A link without a frame lists the frames to choose from. The token is read only from the `FIGMA_TOKEN` environment variable and is sent to Figma only. `--variables` also proposes tokens from the file's variables (Enterprise plans only) in `.ai/design/tokens.proposed.json`, which nothing reads until you rename its tokens to this project's and save it as `tokens.json`.
- **What it does not do**: it does not read the design's meaning. The tokens and the PRD's **UI & design** section are still written, or drafted and reviewed, by a person: colours measured from a picture are approximate, and the census is a starting point.

